---
description: Project continuity is Mental. Orient and hand off via the CLI; never grep OKF.
alwaysApply: true
---

Continuity is Mental. On start/finish of real work, or orientation questions, use the Mental skill.

Run `mental where` then `mental heartbeat --json` (or `mental status --json` when notes are needed). Do not grep `.mental` or `~/.mental`. Do not parse YAML frontmatter yourself.

If `--json` includes `update`, tell the user once this session to run `mental install`. Do not block work.

Mid-chat, not just start/finish: before changing an approach, `mental search "…" --json` for prior decisions. When residue surfaces ("X said…", a concern, "park this", unverified agent output), record `mental attention … --json` immediately — do not wait for the handoff. Interrupted mid-hop: `mental park --resume … --json`. Cross-project overview: `mental pulse --json` (compact rows, no journal dump). Cheap mid-chat reload stays `mental heartbeat --json`. Writes pass `--via cursor` (short client token, never a session id).

If `mental` is not on PATH, try `npx @balacode/mental …`. If that fails, continue the user's coding task and mention install. Missing Mental must not block work.

Never commit Mental data. Never write secrets. Never edit gitignore; tell the user to run `mental doctor`.

If `mental heartbeat --json` includes `data.track.enabled`, follow the Mental Track skill. If tracking is off, do nothing — do not enable it and do not suggest `mental option track on`.

If you invoked `mental` this turn, end the user-visible reply with `🧠 **Mental**` then a markdown bullet list: `- emoji **Kind** › *action* › title`. Read with no title omits the third slot. Never inline. No `────────`. Not `</br>`. Example in the Mental skill. Not a code fence.

On a pasted transcript or a plan-progress question ("where in the plan / what's left?"), use the Mental skill. Cheap reload is `mental heartbeat --json`, not a notes dump.
